      "text": "1. Identify the general form of the sequence: \\(3^0, 3^0 + 3^1, 3^0 + 3^1 + 3^2, \\ldots\\).\n2. The \\(n\\)-th term of the sequence can be expressed as the sum of a geometric series:\n   \\[\n   a_n = 3^0 + 3^1 + 3^2 + \\cdots + 3^{n-1}\n   \\]\n3. Recall the formula for the sum of the first \\(n\\) terms of a geometric series:\n   \\[\n   S_n = a \\frac{r^n - 1}{r - 1}\n   \\]\n   where \\(a\\) is the first term, \\(r\\) is the common ratio, and \\(n\\) is the number of terms.\n4. For this sequence, the first term \\(a = 3^0 = 1\\), the common ratio \\(r = 3\\), and the number of terms \\(n = 4\\). Plugging these values into the sum formula:\n   \\[\n   a_n = \\frac{3^n - 1}{3 - 1} = \\frac{3^n - 1}{2}\n   \\]\n5. Substitute \\(n = 4\\) into the formula to find the fourth term:\n   \\[\n   a_4 = \\frac{3^4 - 1}{2} = \\frac{81 - 1}{2} = \\frac{80}{2} = 40\n   \\]\n\nThe simplified value of the fourth term of the sequence is \\(\\boxed{40}\\).",
